  "question": "Given a string `s` and an array of strings `words`, return an array of the starting indices of all the concatenated substrings in `s`. A concatenated string is a string that exactly contains all the strings of any permutation of `words` concatenated.",
  "explanation": "To solve this problem, we need to identify all substrings in `s` that are a concatenation of all strings in `words` in any order. We will use a sliding window approach to check substrings of `s` with the same length as the total length of all strings in `words`. For each starting index, we will check if the substring is a concatenation of the words. We will use a hashmap to count the frequency of words and compare it with the frequency of substrings extracted from `s`.",
  "solution": "```rust\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\nimpl Solution {\n    pub fn find_substring(s: String, words: Vec<String>) -> Vec<i32> {\n        let mut result = Vec::new();\n        let word_len = words[0].len();\n        let total_len = word_len * words.len();\n        let mut word_count = HashMap::new();\n        \n        for word in words {\n            *word_count.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;\n        }\n        \n        for i in 0..=(s.len() - total_len) {\n            let mut seen = HashMap::new();\n            let mut j = 0;\n            while j < words.len() {\n                let word = &s[i + j * word_len..i + (j + 1) * word_len];\n                if !word_count.contains_key(word) {\n                    break;\n                }\n                *seen.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;\n                if *seen.get(word).unwrap() > *word_count.get(word).unwrap() {\n                    break;\n                }\n                j += 1;\n            }\n            if j == words.len() {\n                result.push(i as i32);\n            }\n        }\n        result\n    }\n}\n```",
  "dry_run": "Let's take an example: `s = \"barfoothefoobarman\"`, `words = [\"foo\",\"bar\"]`. \n1. `word_len` = 3, `total_len` = 6.\n2. `word_count` = {\"foo\": 1, \"bar\": 1}.\n3. Iterate over `s` from index 0 to 8 (since `s.len() - total_len` = 14 - 6 = 8).\n4. For `i` = 0, the substring is \"barfoo\". It matches the words, so `result` = [0].\n5. For `i` = 9, the substring is \"foobar\". It matches the words, so `result` = [0, 9].\n6. Return `result` = [0, 9].",
  "complexity": "Time complexity: O(n*m*k) where n is the length of `s`, m is the length of `words`, and k is the length of each word in `words`. Space complexity: O(m) for storing the word count and seen words."
}
